Prompt:

[BRAND]: {brand name}
[PRODUCT]: {food / drink}
[HEADLINE]: {short bold word}
[INTERACTION]: {liquid drips over type / type dips into liquid / food smears across type / liquid motion moves type}
[ACCENT COLOR]: {main accent color}
[DETAILS]: {ingredients / short copy / hours / address}

Create a 9:16 vertical food editorial poster combining highly realistic food photography with bold typography through one clear liquid or fluid interaction.

Use a warm cream or ivory background, subtle paper texture, realistic food materials, natural lighting and a clean editorial layout. The result should feel like a real café, bakery or restaurant campaign.

The food or liquid must physically interact with [HEADLINE] according to [INTERACTION], rather than simply appearing beside it.

For “liquid drips over type,” let one continuous syrup or sauce flow naturally over the letters with realistic gravity, thickness, surface tension and dripping.

For “type dips into liquid,” place the headline into the same real liquid surface as the food, with one shared immersion line and believable contact between liquid and letters.

For “food smears across type,” let a real spread, jam or sauce be physically dragged across the headline, showing true thickness, transparency, fruit pieces or texture instead of a painted brush stroke.

For “liquid motion moves type,” form the headline from a movable food material such as cocoa powder, then let a real swirl or stirring motion gradually displace it according to the fluid flow.

Keep [HEADLINE] large, simple and readable at thumbnail size. The interaction should be obvious within the first second.

Use [ACCENT COLOR] sparingly with cream, natural food colors and dark neutrals. Add only a few meaningful details such as [BRAND], product name, ingredients, one short line of copy, opening hours or address.

Keep props minimal. Avoid CGI liquids, paint-like textures, digital distortion, fake drips, excessive splashes, generic 3D type, numbered labels, 01/02, Vol. or sparkles.

The final poster should work as a stop-scroll image: one bold headline, one clear liquid action, and one surprising but physically believable Food × Typography interaction.

Do not add a liquid effect to the type — let the real food physically act on it.

Prompt:

[SUBJECT]: {subject}
[TITLE]: {name}
[LINE FIELD]: {concentric / diagonal / spiral / radial}
[BACKGROUND]: {color}
[LINES]: {color}
[TEXT]: {2–4 short lines}
[FORMAT]: 9:16

Create a refined experimental poster where [SUBJECT] emerges from the deformation of a precise geometric line field.

Use one coherent [LINE FIELD] system across the entire image. Keep the geometry clean and regular away from the subject, then let the lines smoothly compress, expand, bend, and flow around its 3D volume.

Do not outline the subject separately. Let anatomy and form appear mainly through deformation and negative space.

For soft forms, use long fluid curves. For muscular or structural forms, use stronger controlled compression and directional tension without drawing individual muscles.

Keep the subject at roughly 70–80% of the composition and add only 2–3 small typography blocks in quieter negative spaces.

Prioritize line quality above everything else: extremely fine, sharp, continuous, evenly spaced, smooth, and geometrically coherent.

Style: Op Art, generative graphic design, modern sculpture, experimental editorial poster, refined minimalism.

Avoid broken or intersecting lines, warped circles, tangled spirals, messy radial centers, jagged curves, excessive realism, wireframe anatomy, sci-fi UI, logos, numbering, starbursts, and sparkles.

Prompt:

[BRAND]: {brand name}
[PRODUCT]: {food / dish}
[HEADLINE]: {short bold word}
[INTERACTION]: {tie a knot / interlock with type / weave over and under / pierce through type}
[FOOD ELEMENT]: {pretzel rope / onion rings / bacon strips / skewer / other suitable food}
[ACCENT COLOR]: {headline color}
[DETAILS]: {ingredients / short copy / hours / address}

Create a 9:16 vertical food editorial poster combining highly realistic food photography with large, professionally designed typography.

Use a warm cream or ivory background, subtle paper texture, clean editorial composition and minimal props.

Keep [HEADLINE] large, flat, sharp and fully readable. The food must remain a real independent object and physically interact with the typography according to [INTERACTION].

For “tie a knot,” let one continuous edible strand move in front of and behind the letters, then form one clear physical knot with believable overlap, compression and contact shadows.

For “interlock with type,” use closed-loop foods such as rings. Let the food loops genuinely interlock with each other and with letter strokes, with opposite front and back relationships at different crossing points.

For “weave over and under,” use several continuous food strips. Alternate them clearly in front of and behind the typography, and allow one or two believable food-to-food crossings to create a real woven structure.

For “pierce through type,” use one rigid food element or skewer as a strong directional axis. Let it pass through letter counters and alternate between the front and back of the headline while remaining continuous from end to end.

Every interaction must have clear three-dimensional depth. Food in front should cast subtle contact shadows; food behind the letters must be genuinely hidden. Keep each food element’s thickness, texture, direction and structure continuous so the viewer can trace its full path.

Do not turn food into letters, do not damage or distort the typography, and do not use fake holes, digital warp, generic 3D effects or decorative overlays.

Use [ACCENT COLOR] with natural food colors and dark neutrals. Add only a few meaningful details such as [BRAND], product name, ingredients, one short line of copy, opening hours and address.

The final image should work as a stop-scroll poster at thumbnail size: one bold headline, one recognizable food structure and one immediately understandable physical interaction.

Keep the typography intact, let the real food lock into its spatial structure.

Prompt:

[Sport / Action]: {Paragliding, freediving, mountain biking, rock climbing...}
[Environment]: {Sky, ocean, forest, cliffs, or another immersive setting}
[Main Headline]: {Your bold English title}
[Typography Surface]: {Inflated fabric, waterline, tree trunks, overhanging rock...}
[Perspective Effect]: {Surface curvature, refraction, multi-plane alignment, inverted perspective...}
[Brand / Color Palette]: {Custom branding and contrasting colors}

Create a cinematic 9:16 adventure sports poster where gigantic typography becomes part of the physical environment.

Capture a professional athlete in a dramatic yet believable action pose. Emphasize realistic anatomy, authentic equipment, natural lighting, and the immense scale of the surrounding landscape.

Design oversized, ultra-bold English typography that interacts directly with the chosen surface. Letters may bend across inflated fabric, refract through water, align across objects at different depths, or stretch along an inverted rock surface.

Use accurate perspective, realistic occlusion, and material-specific distortion. The typography should feel integrated into the scene while remaining clearly readable.

Build a powerful visual hierarchy through scale contrast, dynamic composition, and carefully controlled colors.

Include a compact editorial system with top branding, small project information, vertical side labels, and a meaningful bottom tagline.

Premium outdoor campaign photography, immersive depth, natural textures, and bold visual experimentation.

Avoid floating 3D letters, random distortions, incorrect anatomy, excessive effects, and generic placeholder text.

Prompt:

Create a 9:16 editorial food poster based on the following inputs:

Brand: {brand name}
Food / Drink: {main product}
Headline: {main campaign line}
Supporting text: {product name / ingredients / price / opening hours}
Background color: {light neutral or soft brand color}
Accent colors: {1–2 restrained accent colors}
Typography word: {one short word related to the product}
Composition direction: {vertical / diagonal / circular / crossed / horizontal}

Build the entire poster around the natural shape and direction of the food itself.

Use one clear hero product with highly realistic food photography: appetizing textures, natural highlights, shadows, moisture, crumbs, glaze, melted cheese, glass reflections, fresh ingredients, or other details appropriate to the product. Avoid CGI, plastic textures, excessive retouching, or generic e-commerce photography.

Let the product shape determine how the layout moves. A tall drink can drive a vertical composition, a pizza slice can create a strong diagonal, a round bowl can guide circular typography, and crossed products can form an X-shaped structure.

Use oversized editorial typography as an active part of the composition. The main word should be very large, cropped by the canvas, partially hidden behind the food, split across different areas, rotated, curved, or extended beyond the frame according to the product geometry.

The food and typography must overlap and interact. Do not simply place text beside the product. Let the product cover parts of letters, cross typographic boundaries, break through color blocks, or interrupt the grid so photography and graphic design feel integrated.

Use a clear hierarchy:
oversized display typography,
hero food photography,
main headline,
product information,
price / time / short supporting details.

Keep all supporting copy meaningful and concise. Do not use Lorem Ipsum or random filler text.

Use a restrained color system with a light background, realistic food colors, and 1–2 brand accents. Add only a few graphic elements such as thin lines, dots, open frames, geometric blocks, soft organic shapes, or small information labels.

The composition should feel energetic and asymmetric while still carefully art-directed, with enough negative space to keep the poster readable.

Final style: modern food editorial design, bold typography, dynamic composition, photorealistic food photography, retro-modern branding, clean spacing, playful but professional.

